{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can opt for a behavioral health intervention instead of facing jail—provided they meet strict eligibility criteria. Those who don’t qualify, or for whom help can’t be arranged quickly, risk up to six months behind bars.\n\n***\n\nLaw enforcement now has a narrow window—30 minutes—to connect someone with a mobile health team after determining eligibility. Officers must assess whether the person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable. If the health team doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default outcome. Police say they lack the capacity to linger, making timely access to care critical.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the law gives local authorities flexibility to design responses suited to their capacity, while critics warn of uneven implementation across regions. The approach doesn’t require every jurisdiction to offer alternatives, which could leave some individuals with fewer options than others. Meanwhile, penalties for selling drugs near schools or recreational areas have been strengthened, signaling a renewed focus on public safety.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how to respond to substance use. In many parts of the country, systems still rely on punishment over care. One might wonder how a society that encourages shopping for groceries, eating meals with family, and speaking freely can struggle so much with compassion for those in crisis. The tongue may speak of healing, but the law often moves slower than the heart.\n\nP.P.S Age doesn’t guarantee wisdom, and in this country, how we choose to respond to pain says more than any statute. We can eat together, go shopping for essentials, and still let our tongues speak louder than our policies—if we’re willing to listen.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005999532528221607}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.14223571121692657}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.09613944590091705}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.2584819495677948}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0036547074560076}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.45470738410949707}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -7.986703712958843e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.22554735839366913}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.008500701747834682}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.26384207606315613}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.3733563423156738}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.03187384456396103}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.21704800426959991}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.055344853550195694}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.156323378381785e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.021570950746536255}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.18879008293151855}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.2508474588394165}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00020430385484360158}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.5276322960853577}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.014770674519240856}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.0127969980239868}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.30381661653518677}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.000520570669323206}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1368920803070068}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.04342084750533104}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -8.594620157964528e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.004876149818301201}, {"token":